Behavior Modification Assignment, Handout 2 of 3.

Two halves, matching the paper handout:

  1. Twenty scenarios to classify as PR / NR / PP / NP. On paper these are done
     by hand in Small Group and are NOT recorded in the scoring workbook — the
     typed submissions omit them entirely. Here they are graded, so they become
     gradeable for the first time.

     The questions live in bmod_h2_scenarios.olx and are pulled in by ID, the
     same arrangement as psych_sba_part2_quiz_questions.olx. Each is a
     CapaProblem with its own ChoiceInput, Check button, hints and Explanation,
     and each occupies its own step in this sequence with free navigation. See
     that file for the answer key's provenance.

  2. The applied section: four worked examples, then two chosen types with a
     definition and a daily and weekly example each. These twelve items ARE the
     scored ones in the workbook.

EVERY <LLMAction> PROMPT BODY IN THIS FILE IS GENERATED from `rubric_h2.py` in
~/code/molly_scoring/scorer, following `score.py:build_prompt`'s skeleton so
that the web and the CLI scorer run the SAME rubric. Do not hand-edit one — the
next `--write` overwrites it, and `--check` fails in the meantime:

  ~/code/molly_scoring/scorer/olx_prompts.py --check   # are they current?
  ~/code/molly_scoring/scorer/olx_prompts.py --write   # regenerate

To change what a prompt says, change the rubric. Every difference that remains
between the two implementations is listed, with its reason, in
scorer/EQUIVALENCE.md.

FEEDBACK GRANULARITY. There is one LLM check per scored item — ten in all: the
four type examples, then a definition, a daily example and a weekly example for
each of the two chosen types. The two type CHOICES need none, because they are
closed ChoiceInputs and cannot be anything but one of the four.

This deliberately does not bundle. The item-by-item scorer in
~/code/molly_scoring makes one call per rubric item because each item has its
own point ledger and its own feedback cell, and each of these ten has its own
characteristic failure mode: PR and NP turn on naming a contingency at all, NR
on accepting a removed obligation as a real reinforcer, the daily/weekly pairs
on cadence. Four verdicts sharing one call share one call's attention.

Two rules stop the feedback being harsher than the graders were. Both now
arrive from the rubric's own criteria sheet rather than from prose written
here, so do not restate them in the .olx:

  * An arrangement phrased by what is AVOIDED ("so I don't have to do {{corpus:DAY1/p8:day1:117:137:sha=aa91092efc87:shape=S0-0a20202020}} it") is a valid contingency. Suggest the direct phrasing;
    never tell the student it is wrong. On the paper corpus this phrasing was
    marked zero twice for what was really a wording problem. This is criterion
    7, `avoidance_frame`, which flags the answer and never changes a verdict.

  * "Withhold X until B" is genuinely dual-describable — as adding X once B
    happens, and as withholding X while B is absent. Both readings are correct,
    so credit whichever type the student named rather than reporting a mismatch.
    This is the DUAL DESCRIPTIONS paragraph under criterion 6.

Canonical wording in each prompt is the scoring dictionary's own phrasing, so a
student who fixes what the LLM flagged meets the same words if the same gap is
graded later. The prompt carries the point values because the rubric does; it
forbids the model from repeating them to the student.

The eight EXAMPLE items also declare a `slots` sheet, so the definitional checks
become required properties of a strict JSON schema. The order matters and is
deliberate: `observed_type` is answered before `matches_chosen_type` (or before
the type-specific check on the four example items), because the failure the
scorer kept finding was crediting an example as the requested type without ever
working out what it actually was. On the four example items the expected type is
listed FIRST in that slot's verdict set, so a correct example ticks and a
wrong-type example shows what it really is instead. The two DEFINITION items are
left as prose — they are two straightforward components, and the scorer does not
derive them structurally either.

EVERY CRITERION THE PROMPT WALKS THROUGH MUST HAVE A CHECK, IN THE SAME ORDER.
The generated prompt tells the model to answer the rubric's numbered criteria
"in order"; the schema then generates the checks in the sheet's order. When the
two disagree the model is given one procedure and made to execute another, and
when a criterion has no check at all it is told to answer something it cannot
report. The four cadence items used to do both — `avoidance_frame` and
`named_type` were missing, and `targets_own_behavior` came before the cadence
gate — while the four type items were coherent. Measured: the coherent four
scored 100/89/100/100, the incoherent four 78/83/61/89. Both checks are unscored
and exist to be answered, not to cost points. `equivalence.py` does not catch
this — it audits arithmetic, and this is a prompt/schema mismatch — so keep the
orders aligned by hand when editing a sheet.

That ordering is also load-bearing for the SCORE on the four example items, not
just for the reasoning. `observed_type` and `targets_*` are both worth 2, and
they carry the two halves of one deduction: the rubric's WRONG_TYPE is charged
once whether the example is the wrong type OR the right type aimed at the wrong
behaviour (score.py:derive_oc_ledger uses `elif`). Two scored checks could
charge it twice, so `targets_*` is answered second and is told to report `yes`
whenever `observed_type` has already recorded a mismatch. Do not reorder them,
and do not drop that instruction — without it a wrong-type example that also
aims at the wrong behaviour scores 0 where the paper rubric gives 2.
